# What Vercel uploads for the Next.js build.
#
# Vercel does NOT read .gitignore, so generated native directories and the
# mobile app's own node_modules were being uploaded: 18,003 files against a
# 15,000 limit, which failed the deploy outright.
#
# EVERY PATTERN HERE IS ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH, AND MUST STAY THAT WAY.
# These are gitignore semantics: an unanchored `mobile/` matches a directory
# named `mobile` at ANY depth, so it silently also excluded
# src/app/api/mobile/ and src/app/api/extension/ — which deployed a build with
# no mobile API and no extension API, both 404 in production. Anchoring is the
# whole fix.

# The Expo app: its own node_modules (~60k files) plus ios/ and android/,
# which `expo prebuild` regenerates and which are gitignored already.
/mobile/

# The browser extension — shipped to the Chrome/Firefox stores, not to Vercel.
# NOT to be confused with /src/app/api/extension/, which must ship.
/extension/

# Reel scripts, renders and the render pipeline.
/marketing/

# Brand source art. The web app serves its own copies from public/brand/.
/Logo/
/design_handoff_repoherald_landing/

# Transcripts, not code.
/session-logs/
/SessionHistory1.txt

# Local build output. Vercel builds on its own side; uploading ours is waste.
/.next/
/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
